#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069845} :activity/id #uuid "2c58acda-3ba1-54f9-9113-1e4c28d227a2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048576 :topic/id #uuid "aad98a45-0367-5e41-9f07-676321ac2b21" :topic/math-academy-id 440} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069977 :tutorial/content "To solve an equation that contains multiple rational terms, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. \n\nFor example, consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2x^2}= \\dfrac{1}{2x}-\\dfrac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution because it would make the denominators equal zero.\n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $2x^2,$ $2x,$ and $x,$ so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is \n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(2x^2, 2x, x) = {\\color{black}2x^2}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by ${\\color{black}2x^2}$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}} & =2{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\cancel{2{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{2{x}^{2}}} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2x}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{\\cancel{2x}\\cdot x}{\\cancel{2x}}-\\frac{\\cancel{x}\\cdot 2x}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\ 1 & =x-2x \\\\ 1 & =-x \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x = -1$ is different from $x=0,$ we can accept it as the solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "71bfcc96-f9ce-5b5f-868d-60177e6790d8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3762 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cccb1623-1f88-5a91-a725-a9297c4a42bd" :step/math-academy-id 465010 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069846}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069846 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068880 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {